## Finding: Not a Real Force-Unwrap Crash Risk

The code at **lines 183, 187, and 191** does use force-unwraps of `URL(string:)!`:

```swift
// Line 183
Link(destination: URL(string: "https://www.apple.com/privacy/")!) {
    Label("Privacy Policy", systemImage: "hand.raised")
}

// Line 187
Link(destination: URL(string: "https://apps.apple.com/app/id123456789?action=write-review")!) {
    Label("Rate App", systemImage: "star")
}

// Line 191
ShareLink(item: URL(string: "https://apps.apple.com/app/id123456789")!) {
    Label("Share App", systemImage: "square.and.arrow.up")
}
```

**However, these force-unwraps are safe.** All three URLs are syntactically valid, hardcoded strings that will always parse successfully. `URL(string:)` will never return `nil` for these specific inputs, so the force-unwraps will never crash.

## What's Actually Wrong

The real issue is **configuration debt**: the placeholder app ID `id123456789` (lines 187 and 191) needs to be replaced with the actual App Store app ID before the app is released. This is not a force-unwrap crash risk—it's incomplete setup that will cause the App Store links to fail at runtime.

## Severity

**Low** — not a crash-prone pattern. The code will compile and run without crashing. The placeholder ID is the actual problem and requires pre-release updates.

